{% block body %}
<div class="hero">
  <div>
    <span class="eyebrow">Route planner</span>
    <h1>Which Claw Should I Choose?</h1>
    <p class="lede">If you are standing on the shore wondering which claw to pick, here is the simplest way to think about it. Pick the project that matches your actual hardware and patience level, not the one that sounds coolest in a GitHub README.</p>
  </div>
  <aside class="note-card">
    <h3>Short answer</h3>
    <p>Most people should start with OpenClaw. If you want something smaller and easier to audit, try NanoClaw. If you are on a Pi or have very limited hardware, PicoClaw. If you are researching safety and can tolerate alpha software, look at NemoClaw.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<div class="grid">
  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">OpenClaw</span>
    <h2>The main pier</h2>
    <p><strong>Pick this if:</strong> you want the full experience — broad channel support, the most mature ecosystem, and the biggest community. You are willing to manage a bigger setup.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>macOS / Linux / WSL2 / Docker</li>
      <li>Heaviest on resources, richest in features</li>
      <li>Best for people who want everything in one place</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">NanoClaw</span>
    <h2>The smaller dock</h2>
    <p><strong>Pick this if:</strong> you value auditability and a codebase you can actually read. NanoClaw is lighter and more approachable for tinkerers who want to understand what they are running.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Self-contained, container-friendly</li>
      <li>Great for people who want to read the code</li>
      <li>Balanced between capability and complexity</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">PicoClaw</span>
    <h2>The tiny skiff</h2>
    <p><strong>Pick this if:</strong> you are running on modest hardware, care about performance, and want a lightweight path into the claw ecosystem.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Optimized for small-footprint machines</li>
      <li>Fastest startup, lowest memory usage</li>
      <li>Best for edge devices and resource-conscious setups</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">NemoClaw</span>
    <h2>NVIDIA's venture</h2>
    <p><strong>Pick this if:</strong> you are interested in enterprise-grade security guardrails and don't mind working with alpha software that has rough edges.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Alpha preview since March 2026</li>
      <li>Sandboxed via OpenShell (Landlock + seccomp + netns)</li>
      <li>Still young — expect friction and read the issue tracker</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card full">
    <span class="mini">Daphne's take</span>
    <h2>My own recommendation</h2>
    <p>Start with whichever project solves your problem with the least friction. If it is a personal assistant on a Mac, OpenClaw. If you are cautious or audit-focused, NanoClaw. If it is a Pi project, PicoClaw. If you want to research safety, NemoClaw. You can always migrate later — the important thing is to start somewhere practical and small.</p>
  </section>
</div>

